/* data/learn-c/phy-11-09.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-09.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-09"] = {"10.1":{"s":"Electrostatics is the study of electric charges at rest and the forces and fields they produce. This chapter covers the properties of charge, Coulomb's law, the electric field and its lines of force, electric flux and Gauss' law, the electric dipole, and charge spread continuously over a line, surface or volume."},"10.2":{"s":"Rubbing certain pairs of materials, such as glass with silk or plastic with fur, makes them attract light objects. They have become electrically charged. There are two kinds of charge, positive and negative. Like charges repel and unlike charges attract. A conductor lets charge move freely, an insulator does not. A body can be charged by contact or by induction, and a gold-leaf electroscope detects charge.","w":["Charging by induction leaves the body with charge of the OPPOSITE sign to the rod that was brought near."]},"10.3":{"s":"Electric charge is additive: the total charge of a body is the algebraic sum of its charges. It is quantised: any charge is a whole-number multiple of the charge on the electron, e. And charge is conserved: it cannot be created or destroyed in an isolated system, only moved from one body to another. Like charges repel, unlike charges attract.","f":[{"x":"q = +- n*e","n":"Quantisation of charge: n is a whole number; e = 1.6 × 10^-19 C is the elementary charge.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.3, p.190"},{"x":"n = q/e","n":"Number of electrons transferred when a body gets charge q.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.3, p.190"}],"w":["Charge is carried only by whole electrons, but the unit e is so small that charges look continuous in everyday life."]},"10.4":{"s":"Coulomb's law gives the force between two point charges at rest. The force grows with the product of the two charges, falls with the square of their separation, and acts along the line joining them. It is attractive for unlike and repulsive for like charges. In a medium the force is smaller than in vacuum, by the factor of the permittivity. The forces on the two charges are equal and opposite.","f":[{"x":"F = K*q_1*q_2/r^2","n":"Coulomb force between two point charges a distance r apart; K = 1/(4*pi*epsilon_0) = 9 × 10^9 N m^2 C^-2 in vacuum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.4, p.191"},{"x":"F_med = (1/(4*pi*epsilon))*q_1*q_2/r^2","n":"Force in a medium of permittivity epsilon.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.4, p.191"}],"w":["Put the charges in coulomb and the distance in metre; give the signs only to find the direction.","Coulomb forces are much stronger than gravitational forces between charged particles (about 10³⁶ times for two protons)."]},"10.5":{"s":"Principle of superposition: the force on a charge due to several other charges is the vector sum of the forces due to each one acting alone, as if the others were absent. So with three or more charges, find each Coulomb force separately, with its direction, and add them as vectors. The presence of other charges does not change the force between any two."},"10.6":{"s":"An electric field is the region around a charge in which another charge feels an electric force. The electric field intensity at a point is the force per unit positive test charge placed there; it is a vector, with SI unit newton per coulomb. A point charge's field falls as 1/r². Lines of force start on positive charges, end on negative ones, never cross, and are closer where the field is stronger.","f":[{"x":"E = F/q","n":"Electric field intensity: force F on a small test charge q; unit N/C or V/m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.6, p.195"},{"x":"E = Q/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r^2)","n":"Field of a point charge Q at distance r (directed away from Q if Q is positive).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.6, p.195"}],"w":["The field direction is that of the force on a POSITIVE test charge; on a negative charge the force is opposite.","Electric lines of force never cross each other."]},"10.7":{"s":"Electric flux measures the number of electric field lines passing through a surface. For a small area, it is the field times the area times the cosine of the angle between the field and the normal to the area. It is greatest when the surface faces the field and zero when the field runs along the surface. The SI unit is V m. Total flux through a surface is found by adding the flux through all its small parts.","f":[{"x":"d(phi) = E*dS*cos(theta)","n":"Flux through a small area dS: theta = angle between E and the area vector (the normal to dS).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.7, p.199"},{"x":"phi = integral of E.dS","n":"Total flux through a surface.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.7, p.199"}],"w":["The angle is between the field and the NORMAL to the surface, not the surface itself."]},"10.8":{"s":"Gauss' law: the net electric flux out of any closed surface is the charge inside it divided by the permittivity of free space. It follows from Coulomb's law and holds for any closed surface of any shape. Only charges inside count towards the flux; a charge outside gives zero net flux. The flux is positive for a positive enclosed charge and negative for a negative one.","f":[{"x":"phi = q/epsilon_0","n":"Flux through a closed surface that encloses a total charge q.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.8, p.199"},{"x":"integral of E.dS = Q/epsilon_0","n":"Gauss' law: Q = total charge inside the closed surface.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.8, p.200"}],"w":["Charges outside the closed surface change the field on it but not the net flux through it."]},"10.9":{"s":"An electric dipole is two equal charges of opposite sign, +q and −q, a small distance 2l apart. Its dipole moment is the charge times the separation and points from the negative to the positive charge. In a uniform field a dipole feels no net force but a torque that turns it towards the field. Its field falls as 1/r³; far away, the axial field is twice the equatorial field at the same distance.","f":[{"x":"p = q*(2*l)","n":"Dipole moment: charges +q and -q, separation 2l; unit coulomb metre.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.9, p.201"},{"x":"tau = p*E*sin(theta)","n":"Torque on a dipole in a uniform field E; theta = angle between p and E.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.9, p.202"},{"x":"E = (1/(4*pi*epsilon_0))*2*p*r/(r^2 - l^2)^2","n":"Field at a point on the axis at distance r from the centre.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.9, p.203"},{"x":"E = (1/(4*pi*epsilon_0))*2*p/r^3","n":"On the axis when r is much larger than l.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.9, p.203"},{"x":"E = (1/(4*pi*epsilon_0))*p/r^3","n":"On the equatorial line when r is much larger than l; the direction is opposite to p.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.9, p.204"}],"w":["The torque is largest at θ = 90° and zero when the dipole lines up with the field (θ = 0° or 180°).","Axial field is twice the equatorial field (for r much larger than l), and the two have opposite directions relative to p."]},"10.10":{"s":"When many charges are very close together we treat them as a continuous distribution. Charge on a line is described by the linear charge density, per unit length. Charge on a surface has a surface charge density (per unit area); charge in a volume has a volume charge density. A small element of charge dq is the density times the small length, area or volume, and the field is found by adding the contributions of all elements.","f":[{"x":"lambda = q/l","n":"Linear charge density (C/m): charge q spread uniformly along a length l; dq = lambda*dl.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.10, p.204"},{"x":"sigma = q/A","n":"Surface charge density (C/m^2); dq = sigma*dA.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.10, p.204"},{"x":"rho = q/V","n":"Volume charge density (C/m^3); dq = rho*dV.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 10.10, p.204"}]}};
